module gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation

// THE FLEET LANE'S IN-BAND SMBIOS PRODUCER — gunbc.plans.fleet_machine_authority P3.
//
// ONE ADMISSION, MANY PRODUCERS. P3 admits machines into identity through one admission, A2
// (gunbc.fleet.fleet_machine_unit_binding), fed by capability-specific producers: the out-of-band one is the
// existing gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge `identity_bind_provisional`, which consumes a BMC access
// receipt and reads the FRU board area; the provider-resource one is the existing ProviderResourceBinding, for
// the Hetzner guests. THE PRODUCER HERE IS THE THIRD: in-band SMBIOS over SSH — read the board serial from the
// machine's own firmware, over the transport the fleet already has, for the machines where the other two are
// unavailable. The DGX Sparks have no BMC at all; srv1-srv4 have one whose credential lane the operator holds
// and which this producer must not need in order to admit a flip. srv4 lands first because srv4 is P9's first
// flip.
//
// THE SOURCE VOCABULARY IS NOT MINTED HERE. A reading is a gunbc.machine_intake.subject BoardSerialObservation
// whose source is BoardSerialSource#HostSmbios — one arm of the vocabulary the BMC FRU producer already uses for
// its BmcFru arm (its third arm, PlatformExpectation, is what the seller claimed, which is not a read of the
// hardware and so is not a value any producer here can contribute). A reading joins the same
// `board_serial_observations` list that subject.bind_unit_key folds. A
// parallel "fleet serial source" enum would put one question across two vocabularies
// (gunbc.design_argument::continuous_generalization), and A2's contradiction and duplicate checks would stop
// seeing half the readings.
//
// READ-ONLY IS A SHAPE, NOT A PROMISE. What this producer may run is the closed coproduct
// SmbiosReadInvocation: print a sysfs DMI attribute, decode an SMBIOS table, print a fact about the running
// kernel and machine. There is NO ARM for writing, enrolling, reflashing, resetting or rebooting a host, so an
// observation claiming one does not typecheck, and adding an arm is a visible change to the obligation
// (gunbc.design_argument::how_to_work — the constraint lives where a caller must use it). Privilege is named
// rather than hidden: /sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial is mode 0400 root (the kernel's
// Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-devices-dmi contract, transcribed by extdeps.linux.dmi_id) and dmidecode
// needs the same privilege to open /dev/mem, so the read runs `sudo -n`; `-n` means it would have FAILED
// rather than prompted. srv4's sudoers is NOPASSWD: ALL, so read-only-ness is a fact about WHICH COMMANDS RAN —
// which is what the closed invocation shape, the committed script and the digest-pinned transcript make
// checkable rather than what a comment would assert.
//
// WHERE P3 STANDS, MACHINE BY MACHINE -- RECORDED RATHER THAN REMEMBERED.
// OBSERVED AND ADMITTED: srv4 (P9's first flip, this module's first record) and srv1 (read on the host by the
// lane's manager). Each binds at A2 on its own base-board serial through the fold, the refusals and the transcript
// check below; neither claim is inherited from the other machine's witness.
// WAITING ON A READ, and correctly HostUnitUnobserved rather than refused until one happens: srv3, and
// host-6ce85c. srv3 rejects every credential this session and the lane's manager hold (srv1's own ssh alias
// resolves to a container, which is why srv1's and srv2's records name the manager who actually held the
// keyboard). host-6ce85c was wrongly filed below as a provider guest and is corrected here: machine_board.dag
// gives it identity_stated_board against supermicro_h11ssl_catalog, so it is a bare-metal Supermicro H11SSL with
// a BMC (ATEN), it answers to THIS producer, and what it lacks is a route rather than an arm. Both are owed a
// read, not an invention: no observation is recorded for a machine nobody reached.
// ARRIVING WITH ANOTHER LANE'S OBSERVATION ROWS: srv14 and srv15, whose baseboard serials swift-ram-509's captures
// already hold. Reading those boards again here would be a second read of one board answering one question, which
// is the redundancy the fork in gunbc.integration_concern::inband_smbios_board_parse_fleet_and_arrival_lanes
// exists to collapse; their rows land with that lane's own producer and are consumed, not re-derived.
// NOT THIS PRODUCER'S: the two rented Hetzner guests, host-183527 and host-3dde7f. Their unit identity is a
// provider resource id, and the record for that already exists rather than needing minting here:
// gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_backing declares ProviderResourceBinding (fleet_host_backing.dag:44) and carries it as
// the resource arm of HostBacking#ProviderGuest, grounded by gunbc.fleet.fleet_enrollment (cax41_guest). A rented
// guest has no baseboard to read in-band and no BMC of ours to interrogate, so the board-serial fold above is
// the wrong authority for them and this module says so instead of inventing an arm. (An earlier draft of this
// comment asserted the opposite -- that no such record existed -- on the strength of a grep whose output was
// truncated before the declaration. It is corrected here because a ledger that under-claims its own scope is as
// dangerous as one that over-claims it: it lets a missing observation be mistaken for somebody else's problem.)
// The DGX Sparks (srv9-srv12, no BMC, no route from this container or from srv1) are in this lane's scope and are
// BLOCKED ON ACCESS, which is a different state from 'not this producer's': their reading is owed, the plan names
// in-band SMBIOS as their producer, and P3 records the gap rather than claiming an observation nobody took.
//
// WHAT ONE IN-BAND READING ESTABLISHES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It establishes what this machine's firmware
// reports as its baseboard serial, to a transport principal we control. It does NOT establish that this board
// is not ALSO in another machine we own — W3's failure, two hosts named for one board, which one mechanism
// cannot rule out. So the standing checks everything a single read CAN check: the host that answered printed
// the hostname of the machine we asked (HostAnsweredAsAnotherHost refuses — the W3 red against a wrong host),
// the board product it printed is the board the fleet carries for that machine (BoardModelNotTheFleetRow), and
// the serial is attributed to an admitted read of the field rather than asserted (BoardSerialNotAttributed).
// The BMC FRU read of the same board is the SECOND SOURCE, and for srv4 it is OWED, not agreed: no
// bmc-srv4-admin credential was reachable from the session that took the read. A2 admits on one recorded
// source — the plan does not require two — and if the owed read ever contradicts this one, bind_unit_key
// refuses with BoardSerialContradictory and srv4's cutover stops. That is the acceptance, both ways: a reading
// recorded with A2 still admitting, or a refusal that stops that machine's cutover.
//
// A MACHINE JOINS WHEN ITS READING LANDS. There is no srv1/srv2/srv3/Spark row here because none has been
// read; a machine's row is produced by this module's standing function over a reading, never by typing a
// serial into an admission. Readings carry an EvidenceRef to a committed transcript at a sha256, and
// verify_host_smbios_reading_transcript re-reads that file, refuses on digest drift, and re-parses the serial
// and the hostname from the transcript itself — so a value recorded here that disagrees with the evidence it
// cites turns the witness red instead of passing quietly.

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import std.algebra { string_contains, trim }
import std.list { first_where }
import std.optional { Absent, Optional, Present }
import std.content_hash {
  ContentHash,
  ContentHashComparison,
  ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable,
  ContentHashDifferent,
  ContentHashEqual,
  Sha256Digest,
  Sha256Hash,
  compare_content_hash,
  sha256_hex_digest,
  sha256_hex_of_text,
}
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration, decl_ref }
import std.dissolution {
  BoundDissolution,
  DeclarationAppears,
  DissolutionCondition,
  dissolution_status,
  DissolutionFired,
  DissolutionPending,
  DissolutionStatus,
  DissolutionTrigger,
}
import extdeps.bmc.endpoint { BmcEndpoint }
import extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io {
  Filesystem,
  FilesystemReadRefused,
  FilesystemReadSucceeded,
  filesystem_read_outcome,
}
import extdeps.linux.dmi_id { DmiBoardName, DmiBoardSerial, dmi_id_attribute_path, dmi_id_directory }
import product.host_identity { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.machine_intake.subject { BoardSerialObservation, BmcFru, HostSmbios, UnitIdentityInputs }
import gunbc.machine_intake.receipt { EvidenceRef }
// The 'Key: value' field rule of one SMBIOS/FRU capture section is owned by the arrival lane, not by this
// module; the fleet lane supplies the section and the field name. See type2_base_board_serial.
import gunbc.machine_intake.inband_host_identity {
  inband_section_field,
}
import gunbc.ipmitool_fru_text {
  FruFieldAbsent,
  FruFieldEmpty,
  FruFieldGap,
  FruFieldRepeated,
  FruFieldValue,
}
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity { operator_host_srv1, operator_host_srv2, operator_host_srv4 }
import gunbc.fleet.fleet_intent { srv1_baseboard, srv2_baseboard, srv4_baseboard }

// THE READ-ONLY OBLIGATION, AS A CLOSED SHAPE. Every command an in-band SMBIOS observation ran is one of
// these three, and the arms say what each does to the host: print, decode, print. Nothing here writes,
// enrolls, reflashes, resets or reboots; there is no arm that could claim to.
type SmbiosReadInvocation
  = SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: NonEmptyStr, privileged: Bool }
  | SmbiosTableDecode { table: Int }
  | KernelFactPrint { fact: NonEmptyStr }

// WHAT ONE ATTRIBUTE OR TABLE FIELD CAME BACK AS. The refusing arms carry WHY, so a consumer cannot mistake an
// unreadable field for a read one, or an empty firmware field for a value (gunbc.design_argument::how_to_work:
// a type whose refusing case carries the reason it is unusable beats an Option a caller must remember to
// check). `<unreadable-without-privilege>` is what the read printed where the kernel's 0400 mode met the
// absence of privilege; the blank arm is a firmware field this board never had a value written into — srv4's
// system (type-1) Serial Number and chassis_asset_tag are both blank, so the baseboard serial is the only
// serial this board carries.
type SmbiosFieldReading
  = SmbiosFieldValue { value: NonEmptyStr }
  | SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | SmbiosFieldBlank

// ONE OBSERVATION, AS RECORDED. `observed_at_utc` and `kernel_hostname` are lines the HOST printed, not stamps
// this session asserted; `principal` names who the read authenticated as and with what; `transport` names the
// command that carried it; `transcript` cites the committed transcript at its sha256; `invocations` is every
// command run, in order. The board fields are the readings and the serial is what binds.
type HostSmbiosReading {
  machine: HostIdentity
  observed_at_utc: NonEmptyStr
  principal: NonEmptyStr
  transport: NonEmptyStr
  invocations: List<SmbiosReadInvocation>
  transcript: EvidenceRef
  kernel_hostname: NonEmptyStr
  kernel_release_machine: NonEmptyStr
  board_vendor: SmbiosFieldReading
  board_name: SmbiosFieldReading
  board_serial: SmbiosFieldReading
  // THE SAME SERIAL, SECOND READ PATH. The kernel's own copy at /sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial and the
  // base-board (type-2) table that dmidecode decodes come from one firmware source by two routes, so their
  // agreement is corroboration of the TRANSPORT -- of the SSH session, the shell, and the digest -- and not a
  // second opinion about the board. It is recorded as a field so that the claim is folded rather than
  // asserted beside the capture. Section-scoped by construction: the value is read out of the `smbios.type2`
  // line this capture prints, never by grepping the transcript for "Serial Number", because the system (type-1)
  // table prints its own Serial Number -- blank on every ALTRAD8UD board read so far, on srv4, srv14 and srv15
  // alike -- and a whole-output grep would mint a unit key from the wrong table.
  board_serial_from_smbios_table: SmbiosFieldReading
}

// WHY AN IN-BAND READING DOES NOT ADMIT. Each arm is a refusal this shape can actually reach, carrying the
// reason it is unusable — and a refused reading contributes NO observation, so the machine never joins A2's bound
// set: bind_unit_key refuses it with BoardSerialAbsent, gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_backing's resolve_host_unit calls
// the row HostUnitRefused rather than HostUnitResolved, and that machine's cutover stops. Not HostUnitUnobserved:
// that is the state of a machine nobody has read yet.
type HostSmbiosReadRefusal
  = BoardSerialUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  // Both in-band paths spoke and gave different answers, which is a transport fault or a tampered capture --
  // never a tie to be broken. Nothing is observed and the cutover stops.
  | BoardSerialPathsDisagree { sysfs: NonEmptyStr, table: NonEmptyStr }
  | BoardSerialBlank
  | BoardSerialNotAttributedToARead
  | HostAnsweredAsAnotherHost { asked: NonEmptyStr, answered_as: NonEmptyStr }
  | BoardModelNotTheFleetRow { asked: NonEmptyStr, board_read: NonEmptyStr, fleet_row: NonEmptyStr }
  | BoardModelUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }

type HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding
  = BoardSerialObserved {
      machine: HostIdentity
      observation: BoardSerialObservation
      observed_at_utc: NonEmptyStr
      evidence: EvidenceRef
    }
  | BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: HostIdentity, cause: HostSmbiosReadRefusal }

// IS THE SERIAL ATTRIBUTED TO A READ THAT COULD HAVE READ IT? A value with no admitted read behind it is an
// assertion, and assertions about hardware are what this producer exists to replace. There are two ways the
// field is actually read on Linux and both are admitted: the kernel's sysfs attribute, and the SMBIOS
// base-board table (type 2, where the board's Serial Number lives in the DMI spec).
fn board_serial_read_is_attributed(invocations: List<SmbiosReadInvocation>) -> Bool {
  invocations |> any(i =>
    match i {
      SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: p, privileged: _ } => (p as String) == dmi_id_attribute_path(attribute: DmiBoardSerial)
      SmbiosTableDecode { table: t } => t == 2
      KernelFactPrint { fact: _ } => false
    }
  )
}

// WHETHER AN INVOCATION IS AN IDENTITY READ — the read-only obligation checked against what was actually
// recorded, not only against what the shape allows. The coproduct rules out a write; it does not rule out an
// over-broad read, and `dmidecode --type 11` (OEM strings) is legal output that carries whatever a vendor liked
// rather than an identity fact. So the arms are narrowed here to the DMI identity tree, the two identity tables,
// and the kernel facts this producer names — srv4's recorded invocations are all in this set, and the witness
// says so claim by claim. A new kind of read is admitted by widening this list deliberately, not by the shape
// happening to permit it.
fn smbios_read_is_an_identity_read(invocation: SmbiosReadInvocation) -> Bool {
  match invocation {
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: p, privileged: _ } => starts_with(s: p as String, prefix: dmi_id_directory as String)
    SmbiosTableDecode { table: t } => (t == 1) || (t == 2)
    KernelFactPrint { fact: f } => kernel_identity_fact_is_allowlisted(fact: f)
  }
}

// The kernel facts the producer prints, as data. Each prints one identity fact about the running machine: the
// instant of the read, the name the host answers to, the kernel it runs, its processor count and its memory
// size — the last two corroborating the fleet's model row, and none of them writable from here.
data kernel_identity_facts: List<NonEmptyStr> = [
  "date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ",
  "hostname",
  "uname -srm",
  "nproc",
  "head -1 /proc/meminfo",
]

fn kernel_identity_fact_is_allowlisted(fact: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  kernel_identity_facts |> any(k => k == fact)
}

// THE STANDING. Fold one reading: attribute the read, confirm the host that answered was the machine asked,
// match the board against the fleet's row for that machine, then bind through the subject's own source
// vocabulary.
fn host_smbios_board_serial_standing(
  reading: HostSmbiosReading,
  fleet_board_row: NonEmptyStr,
) -> HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding {
  if !board_serial_read_is_attributed(invocations: reading.invocations) {
    BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: reading.machine, cause: BoardSerialNotAttributedToARead }
  } else if !host_identity_named_as(reading: reading) {
    // W3's red, caught at the producer: the machine that printed this serial is not the machine we asked.
    BoardSerialReadRefused {
      machine: reading.machine,
      cause: HostAnsweredAsAnotherHost { asked: reading.machine as NonEmptyStr, answered_as: reading.kernel_hostname },
    }
  } else {
    match reading.board_name {
      SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: d } =>
        BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: reading.machine, cause: BoardModelUnreadable { detail: d } }
      SmbiosFieldBlank =>
        BoardSerialReadRefused {
          machine: reading.machine,
          cause: BoardModelNotTheFleetRow { asked: reading.machine as NonEmptyStr, board_read: "(blank)", fleet_row: fleet_board_row },
        }
      SmbiosFieldValue { value: name } =>
        if !string_contains(s: fleet_board_row as String, pattern: name as String) {
          BoardSerialReadRefused {
            machine: reading.machine,
            cause: BoardModelNotTheFleetRow { asked: reading.machine as NonEmptyStr, board_read: name, fleet_row: fleet_board_row },
          }
        } else {
          match reading.board_serial {
            SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: d } =>
              BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: reading.machine, cause: BoardSerialUnreadable { detail: d } }
            SmbiosFieldBlank => BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: reading.machine, cause: BoardSerialBlank }
            SmbiosFieldValue { value: serial } => {
              // Corroborated WHEN BOTH PATHES SPEAK, the same shape board_model_must_be_corroborated uses: two
              // values that exist and disagree is a refusal, and a path that printed nothing is not a vote. A
              // machine whose dmidecode is missing is therefore still admitted on the kernel's own attribute --
              // one admitted reading is what A2 asks for -- but it cannot claim the second path, and the record
              // says which of the two it has.
              match reading.board_serial_from_smbios_table {
                SmbiosFieldValue { value: table_serial } =>
                  if table_serial != (serial as String) {
                    BoardSerialReadRefused {
                      machine: reading.machine,
                      cause: BoardSerialPathsDisagree { sysfs: serial, table: table_serial },
                    }
                  } else {
                    BoardSerialObserved {
                      machine: reading.machine,
                      observation: BoardSerialObservation { source: HostSmbios, serial: serial },
                      observed_at_utc: reading.observed_at_utc,
                      evidence: reading.transcript,
                    }
                  }
                _ =>
                  BoardSerialObserved {
                    machine: reading.machine,
                    observation: BoardSerialObservation { source: HostSmbios, serial: serial },
                    observed_at_utc: reading.observed_at_utc,
                    evidence: reading.transcript,
                  }
              }
            }
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

// DID THE HOST PRINT THE NAME WE ASKED FOR? Compared as strings at the brand boundary: the printed kernel
// hostname is a line of host output, the machine is the fleet identity, and the equality is the whole check.
fn host_identity_named_as(reading: HostSmbiosReading) -> Bool {
  trim(s: reading.kernel_hostname as String) == (reading.machine as String)
}

// THE OBSERVATIONS A STANDING CONTRIBUTES TO A2. A refusal contributes the EMPTY list, which is how a machine
// with a refused reading arrives at subject.bind_unit_key as BoardSerialAbsent — and so stays out of the flip.
fn host_smbios_board_serial_observations(standing: HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding) -> List<BoardSerialObservation> {
  match standing {
    BoardSerialObserved { machine: _, observation: o, observed_at_utc: _, evidence: _ } => [o]
    BoardSerialReadRefused { machine: _, cause: _ } => []
  }
}

// BOTH ARMS, JOINED ONCE. The in-band read this module takes and the out-of-band read the FRU lane owes are kept
// apart as producers and joined here, in the one place that becomes A2's list — so a second source cannot arrive
// without being folded, and cannot be folded without being recorded.
fn board_serial_observations_from_both_sources(
  in_band: List<BoardSerialObservation>,
  out_of_band: List<BoardSerialObservation>,
) -> List<BoardSerialObservation> {
  fold(out_of_band, init: in_band, f: (acc, o) => append(acc, o))
}

fn host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs(
  standing: HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding,
  bmc_endpoint: BmcEndpoint,
  out_of_band: List<BoardSerialObservation>,
) -> UnitIdentityInputs {
  UnitIdentityInputs {
    bmc_endpoint: bmc_endpoint,
    board_serial_observations: board_serial_observations_from_both_sources(
      in_band: host_smbios_board_serial_observations(standing: standing),
      out_of_band: out_of_band,
    ),
  }
}

// THE READ PROCEDURE IS ONE DECLARATION, NOT ONE PER MACHINE. Every arm below is one line the committed
// read_smbios.sh prints, in the order it prints them, and it is the SAME 29 invocations srv4's capture and
// srv1's capture ran -- the script is one file at one digest, so a second copy of the procedure would be a
// second place the procedure could quietly drift from the first (gunbc.design_argument::minimize_redundancy:
// a concept is modeled once and every use derived from it). What legitimately differs per machine is what each
// field RETURNED, and that lives in the readings, not here. The unprivileged probe of every attribute the
// kernel ABI exposes under the identity tree comes first -- including /sys/class/dmi/id/product_serial, which
// exists on these kernels, is mode 0400, and came back EMPTY: absent, blank and unreadable are three facts, and
// a capture that conflates the last two can mint a unit key from an empty string. Where an attribute was
// unreadable unprivileged, the privileged read is recorded alongside it (privileged: true) rather than replacing
// it: what an unprivileged observer can see and what privilege sees are both facts about the ABI.
data inband_smbios_read_procedure: List<SmbiosReadInvocation> = [
    KernelFactPrint { fact: "date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ" },
    KernelFactPrint { fact: "hostname" },
    KernelFactPrint { fact: "uname -srm" },
    KernelFactPrint { fact: "nproc" },
    KernelFactPrint { fact: "head -1 /proc/meminfo" },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/bios_vendor", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/bios_version", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/bios_date", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/board_vendor", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/board_name", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/board_version", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_asset_tag", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_vendor", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_serial", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_type", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_family", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_name", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_sku", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_uuid", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_version", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/sys_vendor", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_serial", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial", privileged: false },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/chassis_serial", privileged: true },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_uuid", privileged: true },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/product_serial", privileged: true },
    SysfsDmiAttributePrint { path: "/sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial", privileged: true },
    SmbiosTableDecode { table: 2 },
    SmbiosTableDecode { table: 1 },
]

// THE OUT-OF-BAND ARM IS ONE RULE WITH ONE IMPLEMENTATION, AND EVERY MACHINE JOINS IT AS DATA.
// What is owed for an in-band reading to become a corroborated one is the same obligation for every machine
// with a BMC: read the base-board serial out-of-band from the FRU and record it as a BoardSerialObservation
// whose source is BoardSerialSource#BmcFru. A per-machine copy of that rule was the first shape and was the
// trigger gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization names -- the second local implementation of one rule -- so
// the status and the capability wording live HERE, once, and srv4, srv1 and srv2 (and every machine after them)
// are BmcFruSecondSource records. A machine joins by adding a record, not by adding control flow.
//
// ONE OBLIGATION, TWO STATES, NO ILLOGICAL ONES. The first shape was a record with a board_serial_observations
// LIST and an Optional<EvidenceRef> beside it, and the two optionals were free: 'an observation with no
// capture' and 'a capture with no observation' were both writable, the two rules disagreed about the first
// (status answered Fired off the list while the capture verifier answered NotTaken), and the witness fixtures
// themselves shipped that state. DESIGN.md fail_closed: 'stronger than catching a wrong state is making it
// unwritable', and 'a check restating a constraint the model already carries is a second representation of it'
// -- the BmcFruCaptureObservationAbsent arm and its refusal claim existed only because the type let the state
// be written. So the states ARE the type now: BmcFruOwed carries the machine and nothing else; BmcFruDischarged
// carries THE observation and THE capture it was read out of, one pair that cannot be written half. The emptiness
// that used to be an empty list is the owed variant -- srv4 and srv1 were admitted on the HostSmbios arm alone
// because no bmc-srv4-admin credential was reachable from the sessions that took their reads, and no prose here
// can be read as the FRU arm agreeing with them. When srv2's FRU read landed, its record became a Discharged
// carrying both halves, and three things follow mechanically rather than by editing prose: its owedness below is
// discharged, its A2 inputs carry both arms, and subject.bind_unit_key checks them against each other --
// agreeing they corroborate, disagreeing they refuse with BoardSerialContradictory and that machine's cutover
// stops. An obligation stated as a condition that is already true tracks nothing, so the debt is carried as data.
type BmcFruSecondSource =
    BmcFruOwed { machine: HostIdentity }
  | BmcFruDischarged {
      // THE MACHINE IS THE TYPED IDENTITY, NOT A STRING TYPED AGAIN BESIDE IT, and the observation and the
      // capture it came off travel together: the pair is the discharge, and neither half is writable alone.
      machine: HostIdentity
      observation: BoardSerialObservation
      // THE DISCHARGE POINTS AT BYTES. An obligation discharged 'by a FRU read' is only auditable if the record
      // says which capture discharged it: this names the committed ipmitool output and its digest, and
      // verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture re-reads that file, re-computes its digest and re-parses its Board
      // Serial line against the observation beside it. A present ref whose file disagrees is a refusal, not a
      // discharge -- and 'no capture at all' is no longer expressible here at all; it is the other variant.
      fru_capture: EvidenceRef
    }

// THE DEBT'S STATUS IS THE ARM THAT WOULD REACH A2, AND NOTHING ELSE. Not the variant label: a BmcFruDischarged
// whose cited capture no longer matches its own bytes has not discharged anything, and answering Fired to it
// would be exactly the plausible-but-false output fail_closed refuses. So status asks the ONE route question --
// does an out-of-band observation exist that bmc_fru_admitted_observations will hand to A2? -- and that function
// derives its answer only from a capture that re-verifies: bytes matching the recorded digest, and the printed
// Board Serial equal to the recorded observation. Fired therefore means the serial arrived AND stood on its
// bytes; whether the two arms corroborate or contradict remains subject.bind_unit_key's verdict over the
// folded list, not this function's to pre-empt. The first shape answered off the hand-typed list alone and the
// verifier sat beside it unused; a check nobody routes through is a second representation, so now there is one
// route and this reads the file.
fn bmc_fru_second_source_status(second_source: BmcFruSecondSource) -> DissolutionStatus
  uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  if any(bmc_fru_admitted_observations(second_source: second_source), o => true) {
    DissolutionFired
  } else {
    DissolutionPending
  }
}

// WHAT THE OBLIGATION IS, per state -- the owed arm names what would pay it, the discharged arm names the
// capture that did, because a single wording could not speak truly about both states (bmc_endpoint_capability's
// pattern: a two-state fn whose arms cannot both answer for one state). It reads the machine out of the record,
// so the wording cannot contradict the obligation it describes.
fn bmc_fru_second_source_capability(second_source: BmcFruSecondSource) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match second_source {
    BmcFruOwed { machine: m } =>
      "read {m}'s base-board serial out-of-band from the BMC FRU and record it as a BoardSerialObservation whose source is BoardSerialSource#BmcFru, with the capture committed and cited",
    BmcFruDischarged { machine: m, fru_capture: cap } =>
      "{m}'s out-of-band serial was read from the BMC FRU and stands discharged against the capture at {cap.label}; paying it again would mean a fresh capture, cited the same way",
  }
}

// THE ROUTE A2'S OUT-OF-BAND ARM TRAVELS. This is the only way an out-of-band observation gets into a machine's
// UnitIdentityInputs: the record does not hand over a list it typed by hand -- it asks the verifier what the
// cited bytes actually say, and contributes the observation ONLY when they agree with it (BmcFruCaptureAgrees).
// Anything the verifier refuses -- unreadable file, digest that no longer matches, a printed serial that differs
// from the record, an observation from the wrong source -- contributes the EMPTY arm, the same arm the owed
// variant contributes, so a claimed discharge that cannot re-verify admits nothing beyond the in-band reading
// and leaves the debt Pending above rather than silently counting. host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs below takes
// its out_of_band from here and nowhere else.
fn bmc_fru_admitted_observations(second_source: BmcFruSecondSource) -> List<BoardSerialObservation>
  uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture(second_source: second_source) {
    BmcFruCaptureAgrees { machine: _, serial: _ } =>
      match second_source {
        BmcFruDischarged { observation: o } => [o]
        // Unreachable: only a Discharged record can verify to Agrees. Listed so the match stays closed;
        // handing an owed machine's non-existent arm to A2 would fabricate it.
        BmcFruOwed { machine: _ } => []
      }
    _ => []
  }
}

// ── srv4, the first flip (P9) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// READ ON 2026-11-11T21:29:45Z by session gentle-moth-338, from the srv1 session host, as briansrls@srv4 over
// the in-tree fleet automation key, by the committed script read_smbios.sh; the transcript the host printed is
// srv4_inband_smbios_observation.txt. Why the host that answered is srv4 — and what the reading does NOT prove —
// is written in artifacts/host-observations/srv4-inband-smbios-2026-11-11/00_README.txt. Read plainly: this
// board model is not rare in this fleet (six owned hosts carry the ALTRAD8UD-1L2T row), so the product name and
// the 128 cores corroborate the board and CPU rows the fleet already holds rather than singling srv4 out; what
// ties the answer to srv4 is the hostname the kernel printed, the memory size matching srv4's own out-of-band
// Redfish census, and the serial being unknown to the corpus before this read. The host key was accepted on
// first use, because gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor carries no anchor for srv4.
data srv4_host_smbios_transcript_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(Sha256Digest {
  hex: "060f5af16d891ec7f9bf3e95bb792462967042c21f8a02c441500650af054ac8",
})

data srv4_host_smbios_reading: HostSmbiosReading = HostSmbiosReading {
  machine: operator_host_srv4,
  observed_at_utc: "2026-11-11T21:29:45Z",
  principal: "briansrls@srv4 over the in-tree fleet automation key (~/.ssh/fleet-automation, ssh-ed25519; the private half lives on srv1, not in this repository)",
  transport: "ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -i ~/.ssh/fleet-automation briansrls@srv4 'bash -s' < read_smbios.sh; srv4 resolved to srv4.mynetworksettings.com",
  // The procedure this reading ran, cited rather than restated -- see inband_smbios_read_procedure
  // for why the unprivileged probe comes first and why a privileged re-read is recorded alongside
  // the unreadable unprivileged read instead of replacing it.
  invocations: inband_smbios_read_procedure,
  transcript: EvidenceRef {
    label: "artifacts/host-observations/srv4-inband-smbios-2026-11-11/srv4_inband_smbios_observation.txt",
    digest: srv4_host_smbios_transcript_digest,
  },
  kernel_hostname: "srv4",
  kernel_release_machine: "Linux 6.8.0-147-generic aarch64",
  board_vendor: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ASRockRack" },
  board_name: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ALTRAD8UD-1L2T" },
  // Printed identically by the privileged sysfs attribute read and by the type-2 decode: two read paths over
  // ONE source, not two sources. The unprivileged read of the same attribute printed the kernel's own denial
  // ("Permission denied"), which is why this one arm is privileged: true — the attribute is mode 0400 root in
  // the ABI, so an unprivileged in-band read cannot see the serial at all and a producer that did not know
  // that would record the denial as the machine's identity.
  board_serial: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NG4000900022" },
  // The same string, from the other route: srv4's own `smbios.type2` line carries
  // "Serial Number: NS1NG4000900022" inside the Base Board Information section, while its type-1 line carries
  // an empty one. host_smbios_board_serial_standing refuses if these two ever disagree.
  board_serial_from_smbios_table: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NG4000900022" },
}

data srv4_host_smbios_board_serial_standing: HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding = host_smbios_board_serial_standing(
  reading: srv4_host_smbios_reading,
  fleet_board_row: srv4_baseboard.catalog.model_label,
)

// srv4's OUT-OF-BAND ARM, EMPTY BECAUSE THE READ HAS NOT HAPPENED. The arm is the list A2 folds, and the
// emptiness IS the record: no bmc-srv4-admin credential was reachable from the session that took the in-band
// read, so srv4 is admitted on the HostSmbios arm alone and no prose here can be read as the FRU arm agreeing
// with it. The machine comes from the reading rather than being typed again beside it, and what the debt says and
// how it is answered come from the one bmc_fru_second_source_status / bmc_fru_second_source_capability pair
// applied to this record. When the FRU read is taken its BoardSerialObservation goes INTO
// board_serial_observations, and three things follow mechanically rather than by editing prose: the debt reads
// DissolutionFired, srv4's A2 inputs carry both arms, and subject.bind_unit_key begins checking them against each
// other — agreeing they corroborate, disagreeing they refuse with BoardSerialContradictory and srv4's cutover
// stops. An obligation stated as a condition that is already true tracks nothing (the guarantee ladder asks a
// class below its ceiling to name a next-rung trigger that can actually fail), which is why the debt is data.
data srv4_bmc_fru_second_source: BmcFruSecondSource = BmcFruOwed {
  machine: srv4_host_smbios_reading.machine,
}


// THE CAPTURE STANDINGS, ONE PER MACHINE, AS DATA ON THE ROUTE. The standing is not prose beside the record:
// it is verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture's typed answer over that machine's record, and bmc_fru_admitted_-
// observations consults the same verifier before A2's list is built, so what these say and what A2 received can
// only ever disagree if the file changed between the two reads. srv4's and srv1's are BmcFruCaptureNotTaken —
// nothing was captured, which is a different state from srv2's, and is consumed as one.
data srv4_bmc_fru_capture_standing: BmcFruCaptureStanding =
  verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture(second_source: srv4_bmc_fru_second_source)

// WHAT A2 CONSUMES. gunbc.fleet.fleet_machine_unit_binding folds this into fleet_machine_unit_evidence; the
// serial is not typed there — it arrives through this reading and its standing, and the out-of-band arm arrives
// only through bmc_fru_admitted_observations, never as a hand-typed list.
data srv4_host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs: UnitIdentityInputs = host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs(
  standing: srv4_host_smbios_board_serial_standing,
  bmc_endpoint: srv4_baseboard.bmc,
  out_of_band: bmc_fru_admitted_observations(second_source: srv4_bmc_fru_second_source),
)



// THE PARSE IS ONE QUESTION WITH TWO LANES, RECORDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY FORKED
// (gunbc.design_argument::continuous_generalization). gunbc.machine_intake.inband_host_identity — landing in
// #16336, the ARRIVAL lane: dmidecode under a rescue OS from a committed capture, with NIC attribution,
// feeding gunbc.machine_intake.arrival_converge — answers the same question this module answers for the FLEET
// lane: what board serial and product does this machine's SMBIOS base-board table report. Only the transport
// and the consumer differ. The register row is
// gunbc.integration_concern::inband_smbios_board_parse_fleet_and_arrival_lanes, and this condition fires when
// the arrival lane's parse lands — which is the moment one of the two is deleted rather than both kept honest.
// The trigger names the exact-once 'Key: value' extractor, because that is the narrower of the arrival lane's
// two shared declarations and the last one a call here would need: its companion, the '### <marker>' section
// scanner inband_section_rows, arrives with it (the marker is row data, so a '--type 2' capture needs no change
// on either side). What does NOT retire is this module's sysfs attribute arm — /sys/class/dmi/id/board_serial has
// no SMBIOS table behind it, it is a different read over a different interface — nor the transport and the A2
// routing, which are fleet-lane questions the arrival lane answers differently.
// FIRED, AND DISCHARGED HERE. #16336 landed inband_section_rows and inband_section_field at 9386010b4b3, so
// this condition reads DissolutionFired and the parse above is a call into that extractor rather than a second
// copy of it -- the moment this comment said one of the two would be deleted rather than both kept honest. The
// register row closed as Integrated on that evidence, naming this module's parse as superseded and the arrival
// lane's field rule as the replacement; what the row preserved, and what still lives here, is the sysfs arm with
// its privilege state and the choice of section, both still checked by execution.
data inband_smbios_board_parse_fork_dissolution: DissolutionCondition = BoundDissolution {
  trigger: DeclarationAppears {
    ref: DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "gunbc.machine_intake.inband_host_identity",
      decl_name: "inband_section_field",
      field: WholeDeclaration,
    },
  },
}

// ── the evidence, checked rather than cited ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// WHERE THE TRANSCRIPT STANDS AGAINST THE RECORD. Digest drift, an unreadable file, or a transcript printing a
// different serial, hostname or board model than the row claims — each refuses with the pair that disagrees, so a
// reader sees which of the two is lying instead of a check that passed.
// Each arm carries only values that exist: the two digests being compared, the field as the reading
// recorded it, the field as the host printed it. No arm is ever built from a placeholder -- an unreadable
// file, a digest of a family this module does not compute, or a reading that states no value at all gets
// its own arm rather than a made-up hex or an empty string pushed through a NonEmptyStr refinement
// (the fabricated-citation class, gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder).
type HostSmbiosTranscriptStanding
  = TranscriptAgrees { digest: ContentHash }
  | TranscriptUnreadable { error: String }
  | TranscriptDigestDiffers { recorded: ContentHash, printed: ContentHash }
  // The recorded citation is not a sha256, so it cannot be compared with the sha256 of the bytes. The
  // comparison authority refuses rather than answering false, and this arm says so: an unreadable
  // verification, not a mismatch.
  | TranscriptDigestCrossFamily { recorded: ContentHash, printed: ContentHash }
  // The sha256 the intrinsic printed for the bytes is not a sha256 hex, so the comparison has no
  // answer; reported as such rather than as a mismatch or a pass.
  | TranscriptPrintedDigestUnverifiable { printed_hex: String }
  | TranscriptRecordedBlank { machine: String }
  | TranscriptRecordedUnreadable { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | TranscriptSerialDiffers { recorded: NonEmptyStr, printed: String }
  | TranscriptHostnameDiffers { recorded: NonEmptyStr, printed: String }
  // THE HOST'S OWN KERNEL LINE, COMPARED LIKE THE OTHERS. kernel_release_machine is provenance a reader trusts
  // -- it is what `uname -srm` printed on the host -- and a field typed beside a capture that nothing compares is
  // a field that can say anything. srv1's first record said "aarch64" where its transcript says
  // "Linux 7.0.0-42-generic aarch64" and nothing caught it; that undetected drift is what this arm exists to catch.
  | TranscriptKernelReleaseDiffers { recorded: NonEmptyStr, printed: String }
  | TranscriptBoardNameDiffers { recorded: NonEmptyStr, printed: String }
  // The reading's recorded base-board serial for the SECOND route (dmidecode's type-2 table) against what the
  // type-2 section of the transcript it cites actually prints. Without this arm the second path would be a
  // value typed beside the capture, and host_smbios_board_serial_standing's cross-check would be comparing one
  // typed literal with another -- green no matter what the host said.
  | TranscriptTablePathDiffers { recorded: String, printed: String }

// THE VALUE OF ONE `key=value` LINE IN THE TRANSCRIPT, or "" where the transcript has no such line.
fn transcript_line_value(lines: List<String>, key: NonEmptyStr) -> String {
  let prefix = concat(key as String, "=")
  match first_where(xs: lines, keep: l => starts_with(s: l, prefix: prefix)) {
    Present { value: line } => substring(s: line, start: string_length(s: prefix), end: string_length(s: line))
    Absent => ""
  }
}

// THE BASE-BOARD SERIAL, READ OUT OF THE TYPE-2 SECTION AND NOWHERE ELSE. The capture prints its
// `dmidecode --type 2` output as one `smbios.type2=` line whose rows are `|`-separated, so the section boundary
// is structural rather than guessed: the answer is the single `Serial Number:` inside that section. That scoping
// is the whole point -- this board's SYSTEM (type-1) table prints its own `Serial Number`, empty, and a parse
// that grepped the whole transcript for the key would mint a unit key out of the wrong table. The three ways to
// have no answer stay three (absent-or-repeated, and present-but-empty), the same grain as
// gunbc.ipmitool_fru_text's FruFieldAbsent / FruFieldEmpty / FruFieldRepeated and as
// gunbc.machine_intake.mtjade1_access_observation::decode_smbios_base_board_block, which decodes the same
// firmware field from a UEFI `smbiosview -t 2` transcript; only the token differs ("Serial Number:" against
// "SerialNumber:"), which is row data under the shared scanner this row's dissolution waits on.
fn type2_base_board_serial(lines: List<String>) -> SmbiosFieldReading {
  let section = transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "smbios.type2")
  let rows = map(split(s: section, delimiter: "|"), l => trim(l))
  // THE FIELD IS READ BY ONE RULE, AND IT IS NOT THIS MODULE'S. #16336 landed
  // gunbc.machine_intake.inband_host_identity::inband_section_field, which is exactly the declaration this
  // module registered as the dissolution of its own 'Key: value' parse (see
  // inband_smbios_board_parse_fork_dissolution below and
  // gunbc.integration_concern::inband_smbios_board_parse_fleet_and_arrival_lanes). So the extraction is a call
  // and not a copy: exact key match before the first colon, exactly one row, and present-but-empty held apart
  // from absent and repeated. What stays fleet-lane is what only the fleet answers -- WHICH section (the type-2
  // baseboard, never the type-1 system table, which prints its own empty Serial Number), the '|' row boundary
  // this capture uses, the privilege state of the sysfs arm, and the routing into A2. Those are the obligations
  // the register row preserved, and this module still enforces every one of them.
  match inband_section_field(rows: rows, field: "Serial Number") {
    FruFieldValue { value: v } => SmbiosFieldValue { value: v }
    FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldEmpty { field: _ } } => SmbiosFieldBlank
    FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldAbsent { field: _ } } => SmbiosFieldUnreadable {
      detail: "the transcript's type-2 section carries no 'Serial Number' row at all" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
    FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldRepeated { field: _ } } => SmbiosFieldUnreadable {
      detail: "the transcript's type-2 section carries more than one 'Serial Number' row, not one" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  }
}

// A reading's field as text, for an arm that has to say what the two routes each said. Every branch renders a
// value the field itself carries; nothing here invents a placeholder to fill a comparison.
fn smbios_field_text(field: SmbiosFieldReading) -> String {
  match field {
    SmbiosFieldValue { value: v } => v as String
    SmbiosFieldBlank => ""
    SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: d } => d as String
  }
}

// THE SECOND ROUTE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE BYTES IT CITES. The standing refuses when the two recorded routes
// disagree; that refusal is only worth having if the route being compared came from the transcript, so the
// comparison is made here, where the file has been read and digest-checked. A record that claims a serial the
// section does not print disagrees, and a record that claims the section said nothing when it said something
// disagrees the same way.
fn table_path_disagrees(reading: HostSmbiosReading, lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  let printed = type2_base_board_serial(lines: lines)
  match reading.board_serial_from_smbios_table {
    SmbiosFieldValue { value: v } =>
      match printed {
        SmbiosFieldValue { value: p } => p != (v as String)
        _ => true
      }
    SmbiosFieldBlank => match printed { SmbiosFieldBlank => false, _ => true }
    SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: _ } =>
      match printed {
        SmbiosFieldValue { value: _ } => true
        _ => false
      }
  }
}

// srv1, read on the host by vivid-koi-387 (see artifacts/host-observations/srv1-inband-smbios-2026-11-11/ for
// the transcript, the script digest and who held the keyboard). Same procedure as srv4 -- same script, byte for
// byte -- so the two records differ only in what the machines answered. srv1 agrees with srv4 on every blank
// that matters: product_serial and chassis_serial exist under 0400 and print empty, and the system (type-1)
// table's own Serial Number is empty, so the base-board serial is the only serial this board carries.
data srv1_host_smbios_transcript_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(
  Sha256Digest { hex: "fcb92cf4e5d480a84111113a9d9b0dc6973633749b0ad17934fdb2097ac51d09" as Sha256DigestHex },
)

data srv1_host_smbios_reading: HostSmbiosReading = HostSmbiosReading {
  machine: operator_host_srv1,
  observed_at_utc: "2026-11-11T22:28:21Z",
  // PROVENANCE RECORDED AS IT HAPPENED, NOT AS srv4'S READ HAPPENED. srv4's capture was taken by this session
  // over the in-tree fleet automation key; srv1's was taken by the lane's manager from their own session on the
  // host, because this session's srv1 alias resolves to its own container and no credential here reaches srv1.
  // The two records are therefore allowed to differ in exactly the fields that describe who ran what -- making
  // them match would have been writing a command nobody ran (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed: no plausible
  // output is fabricated). The procedure, the script digest and the transcript digest are the parts that are
  // genuinely identical, and those are the parts the code checks.
  principal: "briansrls@srv1, read ON the host by vivid-koi-387 (the lane's manager) with passwordless sudo, rc=0 -- not by this session: srv1's ssh alias in this container resolves to the container itself, so this session holds no principal on srv1 and names none it did not use",
  transport: "ssh briansrls@srv1 'bash -s' < read_smbios.sh, run from vivid-koi-387's session on srv1; the script is byte-identical to srv4's (sha256 12a573676ef2112aa2bc85aefe18be95cf06459cf73c98db48e5ba2c548a2d44), so both captures ran the one procedure while the record of who ran it differs",
  // The same procedure srv4 ran, cited from the one declaration of it.
  invocations: inband_smbios_read_procedure,
  transcript: EvidenceRef {
    label: "artifacts/host-observations/srv1-inband-smbios-2026-11-11/srv1_inband_smbios_observation.txt",
    digest: srv1_host_smbios_transcript_digest,
  },
  kernel_hostname: "srv1",
  kernel_release_machine: "Linux 7.0.0-42-generic aarch64",
  board_vendor: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ASRockRack" },
  board_name: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ALTRAD8UD-1L2T" },
  board_serial: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NF9000200201" },
  // Read out of srv1's own smbios.type2 section by type2_base_board_serial; the verifier refuses the record if
  // that section ever says something else, which is what makes this more than a second copy of the line above.
  board_serial_from_smbios_table: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NF9000200201" },
}

data srv1_host_smbios_board_serial_standing: HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding = host_smbios_board_serial_standing(
  reading: srv1_host_smbios_reading,
  fleet_board_row: srv1_baseboard.catalog.model_label,
)

// srv1 joins the one owedness rule above as data: an empty out-of-band arm because the read has not happened,
// its status and its capability wording coming from bmc_fru_second_source_status /
// bmc_fru_second_source_capability exactly as srv4's do.
data srv1_bmc_fru_second_source: BmcFruSecondSource = BmcFruOwed {
  machine: srv1_host_smbios_reading.machine,
}

data srv1_bmc_fru_capture_standing: BmcFruCaptureStanding =
  verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture(second_source: srv1_bmc_fru_second_source)

data srv1_host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs: UnitIdentityInputs = host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs(
  standing: srv1_host_smbios_board_serial_standing,
  bmc_endpoint: srv1_baseboard.bmc,
  out_of_band: bmc_fru_admitted_observations(second_source: srv1_bmc_fru_second_source),
)

// ── srv2, the first machine whose out-of-band arm is DISCHARGED ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// srv1 and srv4 each stand on one in-band reading with the BMC arm honestly owed. srv2 is the first machine in
// this lane where both channels answered and agreed, so it exercises the half of the model the other two could
// only describe: subject.bind_unit_key now receives two observations and must corroborate them rather than
// admit one. Every value below was read out of the verified capture by the generator rather than typed beside
// it -- the transcript digest, the hostname the kernel printed, the os line, the board fields, and the serial on
// both read paths -- and the out-of-band observation cites the ipmitool capture that backs it, which
// verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture re-reads and re-parses rather than taking on trust.
data srv2_host_smbios_transcript_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(
  Sha256Digest { hex: "48d1816430d19b7e904a74b83c8317ba586e562b367078da15f3a2cd28bffe9e" as Sha256DigestHex },
)

data srv2_bmc_fru_capture_digest: ContentHash = Sha256Hash(
  Sha256Digest { hex: "befa386348857b2416680142de9bdf1622cd996f7918027c709827fe188f3fe5" as Sha256DigestHex },
)

data srv2_host_smbios_reading: HostSmbiosReading = HostSmbiosReading {
  machine: operator_host_srv2,
  observed_at_utc: "2026-11-12T11:16:56Z",
  // PROVENANCE AS IT HAPPENED. This session holds no principal on srv2 -- every credential here is refused by
  // it -- so the record names the manager's session on srv1, which hopped once to srv2 as the unprivileged login
  // user. The script was NOT wrapped in sudo: it calls sudo -n internally at the four places the ABI needs it,
  // and a wrapper would have made the unprivileged probe print a value instead of `Permission denied`, collapsing
  // two distinct facts into one the record could no longer tell apart.
  principal: "briansrls@srv2, read over one ssh hop from vivid-koi-387's session on srv1 (the lane's manager), as the unprivileged login user with rc=0 -- the script self-privileges with sudo -n internally, so this was not wrapped in sudo and the unprivileged-vs-privileged distinction in the capture is the host's own answer, not a wrapper's",
  transport: "ssh -o BatchMode=yes briansrls@srv2 'bash -s' < read_smbios.sh, run FROM vivid-koi-387's session on srv1 with a local stdout redirect so the bytes are the host's own; byte-identical script to srv4's and srv1's (sha256 12a573676ef2112aa2bc85aefe18be95cf06459cf73c98db48e5ba2c548a2d44, cmp-verified across all three directories), so three machines ran the one procedure while the record of who ran it differs",
  // The same procedure, cited from the one declaration of it -- srv2 adds no read of its own.
  invocations: inband_smbios_read_procedure,
  transcript: EvidenceRef {
    label: "artifacts/host-observations/srv2-inband-smbios-2026-11-12/srv2_inband_smbios_observation.txt",
    digest: srv2_host_smbios_transcript_digest,
  },
  kernel_hostname: "srv2",
  kernel_release_machine: "Linux 6.8.0-147-generic aarch64",
  board_vendor: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ASRockRack" },
  board_name: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "ALTRAD8UD-1L2T" },
  board_serial: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NF9000200179" },
  // Read out of srv2's own smbios.type2 section by type2_base_board_serial -- which is the arrival lane's field
  // rule called, not a fleet-side copy of it -- and the verifier refuses the record if that section says anything
  // else, which is what keeps the two paths from being two hand-typed copies of each other.
  board_serial_from_smbios_table: SmbiosFieldValue { value: "NS1NF9000200179" },
}

data srv2_host_smbios_board_serial_standing: HostSmbiosBoardSerialStanding = host_smbios_board_serial_standing(
  reading: srv2_host_smbios_reading,
  fleet_board_row: srv2_baseboard.catalog.model_label,
)

// THE DISCHARGE, AS DATA. The same list that is empty for srv4 and srv1 carries one BmcFru observation here, so
// bmc_fru_second_source_status -- one function, unchanged -- answers Fired for srv2 and Pending for the other
// two off the list alone. The serial is the one the BMC's own FRU printed, taken in-band over KCS from srv2
// itself, and it is the same serial both in-band paths printed; the agreement is not asserted here, it is
// recomputed from the cited capture and, on the A2 side, checked by subject.bind_unit_key.
data srv2_bmc_fru_second_source: BmcFruSecondSource = BmcFruDischarged {
  machine: srv2_host_smbios_reading.machine,
  observation: BoardSerialObservation { source: BmcFru, serial: "NS1NF9000200179" },
  fru_capture: EvidenceRef {
    label: "artifacts/host-observations/srv2-inband-smbios-2026-11-12/srv2_ipmitool_fru_print_0.txt",
    digest: srv2_bmc_fru_capture_digest,
  },
}

data srv2_bmc_fru_capture_standing: BmcFruCaptureStanding =
  verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture(second_source: srv2_bmc_fru_second_source)

data srv2_host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs: UnitIdentityInputs = host_smbios_unit_identity_inputs(
  standing: srv2_host_smbios_board_serial_standing,
  bmc_endpoint: srv2_baseboard.bmc,
  out_of_band: bmc_fru_admitted_observations(second_source: srv2_bmc_fru_second_source),
)

// THE MACHINES THIS PRODUCER OWES A READ AND CANNOT YET GET ONE, RECORDED AS DATA. Three states must never be
// collapsed into one another: READ AND ADMITTED (srv4, srv1, srv2, each with a standing its capture corroborates),
// READ AND REFUSED (no machine today -- that is A2's BoardSerialAbsent path, and it stops that machine's cutover
// rather than proceeding), and NOT READ (the list below). The third is the state an author can fake with prose,
// because a machine nobody reached can be made to look handled by writing a paragraph about it, and a reader
// cannot tell a plan from a receipt. So each deferral carries what it is owed, the blocker as it was MEASURED
// rather than as it was assumed, who decided to wait, and a dissolution whose trigger is this module's own reading
// declaration appearing -- the same bound shape the retired fork above uses, so dissolution_status can answer it
// (Pending, not the Unbound that a description string would silently become), and it fires on the thing that
// actually discharges the debt: a reading.
type ObservationDeferral = ObservationDeferred {
  machine: HostIdentity,
  owed: NonEmptyStr,
  blocker: NonEmptyStr,
  decided: NonEmptyStr,
  restore_when: DissolutionCondition,
}

// ONE RULE, SO A FOURTH MACHINE JOINS AS DATA. srv2's landing is the proof: it was added as a record, not as a
// new arm of anything. These six are the same shape with the reading absent.
fn observation_deferral_status(
  deferral: ObservationDeferral,
  present_decls: List<DeclarationRef>,
) -> DissolutionStatus {
  dissolution_status(condition: deferral.restore_when, present_decls: present_decls)
}

data observation_deferrals: List<ObservationDeferral> = [
  // srv3 is the sibling of srv1 and srv2 -- same board family, BMC in the plan -- and it is the one machine here
  // that is reachable at all and still refuses: it answers, and rejects the credential.
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_srv3,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH, and the BMC FRU cross-check its fleet_intent row implies",
    // Measured twice, from this container and from srv1 (the latter at the manager's request, 2026-11-12): the
    // host answers and then refuses every credential held, so this is an access decision and not an outage.
    blocker: "ssh briansrls@srv3 and node@srv3, with and without the fleet automation key, from this container and again from srv1: Permission denied (publickey,password). No BMC credential is held either.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "srv3_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
  // The DGX Sparks. The plan names in-band SMBIOS as THEIR producer precisely because these units have no BMC,
  // so unlike srv3 there is no second channel to fall back on: no route, no unit key.
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_srv9,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH -- the only producer these units have, having no BMC",
    blocker: "No route to host from srv1 to 192.168.1.236 (and .237-.239); .235 refuses port 22. No route from this container either.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "srv9_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_srv10,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH -- the only producer these units have, having no BMC",
    blocker: "No route to host from srv1 to 192.168.1.237. No route from this container either.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "srv10_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_srv11,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH -- the only producer these units have, having no BMC",
    blocker: "No route to host from srv1 to 192.168.1.238. No route from this container either.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "srv11_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_srv12,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH -- the only producer these units have, having no BMC",
    blocker: "No route to host from srv1 to 192.168.1.239. No route from this container either.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "srv12_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
  // NOT a provider guest, whatever an earlier draft of this file said: machine_board.dag binds it to
  // supermicro_h11ssl_catalog, which makes it bare metal and therefore this producer's.
  ObservationDeferred {
    machine: operator_host_6ce85c,
    owed: "in-band SMBIOS base-board serial over SSH, and the ATEN BMC's FRU cross-check once a route exists",
    blocker: "No route or principal held from this container or from srv1.",
    decided: "operator, 2026-11-12: Defer, recorded blocked-on-access rather than observed",
    restore_when: BoundDissolution {
      trigger: DeclarationAppears {
        ref: decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_smbios_observation", decl_name: "host_6ce85c_host_smbios_reading"),
      },
    },
  },
]

// ASK THE CAPTURE FOR A FIELD THE RECORD DOES NOT CARRY. The record keeps the fields A2 and the standing need;
// the transcript keeps everything the script printed. When a claim has to check something the record does not
// hold -- that two sibling boards' UUIDs are genuinely different captures, say -- it goes to the bytes through
// here rather than to a value typed beside them.
fn capture_field(reading: HostSmbiosReading, key: NonEmptyStr) -> String uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let read = Filesystem.Read(path: reading.transcript.label as String)
  match filesystem_read_outcome(content: read.content, success: read.success, error: read.error) {
    FilesystemReadRefused { error: _ } => ""
    FilesystemReadSucceeded { content: text } =>
      transcript_line_value(lines: map(split(s: text, delimiter: "\n"), l => trim(s: l)), key: key)
  }
}

// THE OUT-OF-BAND ARM IS CHECKED AGAINST BYTES, NOT BESIDE THEM. srv4's and srv1's owedness has no capture to
// check, and this module says so with an arm of its own rather than letting an empty list look like a verified
// discharge; srv2's does, and its cited file is re-read, its digest re-computed through the repo's one comparison
// authority, and its Board Serial re-parsed. The parse is the ARRIVAL LANE'S rule again -- inband_section_field --
// called with this capture's key, not a fleet-side copy: one 'Key: value' extractor in the fleet, whatever file
// it is reading. A record that cites a capture it never read out of, a file whose bytes no longer match the
// digest it records, and a Board Serial that contradicts the observation are three different failures and get
// three different answers, because 'the second source disagrees' has to be locatable when it stops a cutover.
type BmcFruCaptureStanding =
    // The record is the owed variant: nothing was captured, so there is nothing to corroborate and nothing to
    // deny. This is srv4's and srv1's state, reached by the TYPE of their record rather than by an absent field.
    BmcFruCaptureNotTaken { machine: HostIdentity }
  | BmcFruCaptureUnreadable { error: String }
  | BmcFruCapturePrintedDigestUnverifiable { printed_hex: String }
  | BmcFruCaptureDigestDiffers { recorded: ContentHash, printed: ContentHash }
  | BmcFruCaptureDigestCrossFamily { recorded: ContentHash, printed: ContentHash }
  // A discharge can cite any committed file, so 'the capture you cited is not an ipmitool FRU print' is a
  // state the coproduct still allows and this check exists for. Its old neighbour 'a citation with no recorded
  // observation' does not: the discharged variant carries the observation with the capture, so that refusal --
  // and the arm it lived in -- were deleted rather than kept as a second representation of the type.
  | BmcFruCaptureWrongSource { machine: HostIdentity }
  | BmcFruCaptureSerialGap { detail: String }
  | BmcFruCaptureSerialDiffers { recorded: NonEmptyStr, printed: String }
  | BmcFruCaptureAgrees { machine: HostIdentity, serial: NonEmptyStr }

fn verify_bmc_fru_board_serial_capture(second_source: BmcFruSecondSource) -> BmcFruCaptureStanding
  uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match second_source {
    BmcFruOwed { machine: m } => BmcFruCaptureNotTaken { machine: m }
    BmcFruDischarged { machine: m, observation: o, fru_capture: ref } => {
      let read = Filesystem.Read(path: ref.label as String)
      match filesystem_read_outcome(content: read.content, success: read.success, error: read.error) {
        FilesystemReadRefused { error: e } => BmcFruCaptureUnreadable { error: e }
        FilesystemReadSucceeded { content: text } =>
          verify_bmc_fru_capture_text(machine: m, observation: o, ref: ref, text: text)
      }
    }
  }
}

fn verify_bmc_fru_capture_text(
  machine: HostIdentity,
  observation: BoardSerialObservation,
  ref: EvidenceRef,
  text: String,
) -> BmcFruCaptureStanding {
  let printed_hex = sha256_hex_of_text(text: text)
  match sha256_hex_digest(hex: printed_hex) {
    Absent => BmcFruCapturePrintedDigestUnverifiable { printed_hex: printed_hex }
    Present { value: printed_digest } => {
      let printed = Sha256Hash(printed_digest)
      match compare_content_hash(left: ref.digest, right: printed) {
        ContentHashDifferent => BmcFruCaptureDigestDiffers { recorded: ref.digest, printed: printed }
        ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable =>
          BmcFruCaptureDigestCrossFamily { recorded: ref.digest, printed: printed }
        ContentHashEqual => compare_bmc_fru_recorded_against_print(machine: machine, observation: observation, text: text)
      }
    }
  }
}

fn compare_bmc_fru_recorded_against_print(
  machine: HostIdentity,
  observation: BoardSerialObservation,
  text: String,
) -> BmcFruCaptureStanding {
  // The source must be the FRU arm of the shared vocabulary. A discharged record whose observation came from
  // somewhere else -- an in-band copy pasted under the FRU's name, which is the mistake two sibling ASRock
  // boards invite -- is not a second source, and no agreement with the bytes makes it one.
  if observation.source != BmcFru {
    BmcFruCaptureWrongSource { machine: machine }
  } else {
    let rows = map(split(s: text, delimiter: "\n"), l => trim(s: l))
    match inband_section_field(rows: rows, field: "Board Serial") {
      FruFieldValue { value: printed } => {
        if printed == (observation.serial as String) {
          BmcFruCaptureAgrees { machine: machine, serial: observation.serial }
        } else {
          BmcFruCaptureSerialDiffers { recorded: observation.serial, printed: printed }
        }
      }
      FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldEmpty { field: _ } } =>
        BmcFruCaptureSerialGap { detail: "the capture prints 'Board Serial' with no value" }
      FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldAbsent { field: _ } } =>
        BmcFruCaptureSerialGap { detail: "the capture carries no 'Board Serial' row at all" }
      FruFieldGap { gap: FruFieldRepeated { field: _ } } =>
        BmcFruCaptureSerialGap { detail: "the capture carries more than one 'Board Serial' row, not one" }
    }
  }
}

// THE BOARD MODEL IS CORROBORATED ONLY WHEN THE RECORD CLAIMS ONE. A blank or unreadable model has nothing to
// compare against, and host_smbios_board_serial_standing refuses those anyway — inventing a comparison for them
// would turn a refusal into a pass. Where the record does claim a value, it has to come back from the host's own
// bytes: the model is the field the standing cross-checks against the fleet's row and the field the flip's
// discrimination between six identical ALTRAD8UD boards leans on, so it is the last one that should be allowed to
// rest on something typed beside the capture. A missing line prints as "" and therefore disagrees.
fn board_model_must_be_corroborated(reading: HostSmbiosReading) -> Bool {
  match reading.board_name {
    SmbiosFieldValue { value: _ } => true
    SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: _ } => false
    SmbiosFieldBlank => false
  }
}

// THE KERNEL LINE THE HOST PRINTED, NOT A STAMP SOMEONE REMEMBERED. read_smbios.sh prints os=$(uname -srm) on
// the machine itself, so the record's release string has a line in the bytes to answer to.
fn kernel_release_disagrees(reading: HostSmbiosReading, lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "os")) != (reading.kernel_release_machine as String)
}

fn board_model_recorded(reading: HostSmbiosReading) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match reading.board_name {
    SmbiosFieldValue { value: v } => v
    SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: d } => d
    SmbiosFieldBlank => "<blank>"
  }
}

fn board_model_disagrees(reading: HostSmbiosReading, lines: List<String>) -> Bool {
  board_model_must_be_corroborated(reading: reading)
    && (trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "dmi.board_name")) != (board_model_recorded(reading: reading) as String))
}

// RE-READ THE COMMITTED TRANSCRIPT. The witness runs this; it is what makes the sha256 in the EvidenceRef do
// work rather than decorate the row. Digest first, then the serial, then the name the host answers to, then the
// board model it reports — each refusal carrying the pair that disagrees, so a reader sees which of the two is
// lying instead of getting a check that passed.
fn verify_host_smbios_reading_transcript(reading: HostSmbiosReading) -> HostSmbiosTranscriptStanding
  uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let read = Filesystem.Read(path: reading.transcript.label as String)
  match filesystem_read_outcome(content: read.content, success: read.success, error: read.error) {
    FilesystemReadRefused { error: e } => TranscriptUnreadable { error: e }
    FilesystemReadSucceeded { content: text } => verify_against_transcript_text(reading: reading, text: text)
  }
}

// THE DIGEST GATE, through the repo's one comparison authority. compare_content_hash answers
// Equal / Different / CrossFamilyIncomparable and refuses to collapse a cross-family pair into a silent
// false, so this module neither invents a hex for a digest it cannot name nor reports "differs" for two
// digests that were never comparable. The sha256 the intrinsic prints is validated on the way into a
// Sha256Digest rather than refinement-cast, so an unverifiable print is an answer of its own.
fn verify_against_transcript_text(reading: HostSmbiosReading, text: String) -> HostSmbiosTranscriptStanding {
  let printed_hex = sha256_hex_of_text(text: text)
  match sha256_hex_digest(hex: printed_hex) {
    Absent => TranscriptPrintedDigestUnverifiable { printed_hex: printed_hex }
    Present { value: printed_digest } => {
      let printed = Sha256Hash(printed_digest)
      match compare_content_hash(left: reading.transcript.digest, right: printed) {
        ContentHashDifferent => TranscriptDigestDiffers { recorded: reading.transcript.digest, printed: printed }
        ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable =>
          TranscriptDigestCrossFamily { recorded: reading.transcript.digest, printed: printed }
        ContentHashEqual => compare_recorded_fields_against_print(
          reading: reading,
          lines: split(s: text, delimiter: "\n"),
        )
      }
    }
  }
}

// FIELD BY FIELD, with the reading's own state deciding the shape. A reading that states no value -- blank
// or unreadable -- has nothing to corroborate, and says so; only a value that exists is ever compared with
// a print, so no arm here pushes an empty string through a NonEmptyStr refinement. Every value an arm
// carries is either the reading's own field or the host's own line.
fn compare_recorded_fields_against_print(
  reading: HostSmbiosReading,
  lines: List<String>,
) -> HostSmbiosTranscriptStanding {
  match reading.board_serial {
    SmbiosFieldBlank => TranscriptRecordedBlank { machine: reading.machine }
    SmbiosFieldUnreadable { detail: d } => TranscriptRecordedUnreadable { detail: d }
    SmbiosFieldValue { value: v } => {
      let printed_serial = trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "dmi.board_serial"))
      if printed_serial != (v as String) {
        TranscriptSerialDiffers { recorded: v, printed: printed_serial }
      } else {
        let printed_hostname = trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "kernel_hostname"))
        if printed_hostname != (reading.kernel_hostname as String) {
          TranscriptHostnameDiffers { recorded: reading.kernel_hostname, printed: printed_hostname }
        } else if kernel_release_disagrees(reading: reading, lines: lines) {
          TranscriptKernelReleaseDiffers {
            recorded: reading.kernel_release_machine,
            printed: trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "os")),
          }
        } else if board_model_disagrees(reading: reading, lines: lines) {
          TranscriptBoardNameDiffers {
            recorded: board_model_recorded(reading: reading),
            printed: trim(s: transcript_line_value(lines: lines, key: "dmi.board_name")),
          }
        } else if table_path_disagrees(reading: reading, lines: lines) {
          TranscriptTablePathDiffers {
            recorded: smbios_field_text(field: reading.board_serial_from_smbios_table),
            printed: smbios_field_text(field: type2_base_board_serial(lines: lines)),
          }
        } else {
          // The digest gate already passed, so this is the record's own cited digest, not a recomputed one.
          TranscriptAgrees { digest: reading.transcript.digest }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
